  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a-b=7 \\\\ \\frac{a}{5}-\\frac{2b}{5}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a-b=7 \\\\ \\frac{a}{5}-\\frac{2b}{5}=1 & |\\,\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}2a-b=7 \\\\ a-2b=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-2b & =5 \\\\ a & =5+2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=5+2b$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-b & =7 \\\\ 2(5+2b)-b & =7 \\\\ 10+4b-b & =7 \\\\ 3b & =-3 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $a,$ we substitute $b=-1$ into the equation $a=5+2b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =5+2b \\\\  & =5+2(-1) \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=3,\\,b=-1.$",
